WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.103
A function pairs each allowed input with exactly one output

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:07.435
This rule triples the input and then subtracts two

00:00:07.435 --> 00:00:14.100
The letter x marks an input position; it does not mean that we must solve for x immediately.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.949
Evaluating the function means supply an input and follow its rule

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:25.324
Replacing x by two gives output four, represented by the point two, four

00:00:25.324 --> 00:00:30.700
The first coordinate records the supplied input and the second records the result.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.207
An entire expression can enter the input slot

00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:41.620
Replace every x with the whole quantity t plus one, using parentheses to protect its grouping

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:48.033
Distributing three then gives three t plus one, an output expression rather than a solved input.

00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:51.676
Solving reverses what is known

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:58.235
Specifying output seven asks which input reaches that height; here the input is three

00:00:58.235 --> 00:01:06.200
Evaluation, symbolic substitution, and solving are related tasks, but their requested answers are different kinds of objects.
